<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Boulogne</h1><p class="st-meta">896 CE – 1477 CE (approx.) · capital Boulogne-sur-Mer</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Channel-coast county whose counts fought at Hastings, ruled Crusader Jerusalem and married into the English throne.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> took shape in the late 9th and early 10th centuries under counts linked to the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalulf-count-of-boulogne">Adalulf</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-margrave-of-flanders">Baldwin II of Flanders</a>, held it in the 910s. The port's position on the shortest crossing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> shaped its history from the start.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts drew wealth from the port, cross-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">Channel</a> trade and lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eustace-ii-count-of-boulogne">Eustace II</a> fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Hastings</a> in 1066, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> and Baldwin I led the First Crusade and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-i-countess-of-boulogne">Countess Matilda</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">Stephen of England</a>. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaud-de-dammartin">Renaud de Dammartin</a> fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Bouvines</a> in 1214 and lost his lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The county passed through the houses of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-auvergne">Auvergne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour</a> before the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a> took it in 1435. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold's</a> death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> seized it in 1477 and placed it under the Virgin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, holding it directly for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-boulogne.jpg" alt="County of Boulogne" width="320" height="307"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 12:09, 2 May 2008 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgundian_northern_France_1465%E2%80%931477.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>896 CE – 1477 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Boulogne-sur-Mer</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eustace II of Boulogne, Matilda of Boulogne, Renaud de Dammartin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Boulogne</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1186&amp;focus=county-of-boulogne" aria-label="County of Boulogne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4717 417 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Boulogne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5037 673L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5037 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5